@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, tool list, thinking level, model endpoint, timeout,
  project root, general agent, fallback agent,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  child host, parent model,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, event log, parent session id, sessions folder, driver files,
}

component TaskCore {
  goal {
    Run each *task* from an agent definition and a brief on one child, keep it
    in a
    durable task record, and return one *result*.
  }

  state {
    A task holds a *task id*, the frozen agent definition it was created from,
    a
    *brief* and one *child run*. A task id is sz- followed by six base32
    characters. The brief is the one self-contained text that the child
    receives,
    and the child sees nothing else of the parent conversation.

    A task also holds its *enqueue time*, the time it was created, and, once
    its
    caller has released its child run, its *start time*, the time of that
    release.

    A child run has a *child run state*. It is Pending until its caller
    releases it, then moves through Starting and Live to Settled, and it can
    also end Failed or Cancelled.

    A failed child run carries one *failure kind*, whose value is timeout, crashed,
    error
    or skill_mismatch. A cancelled child run carries no failure kind.

    A result has a status of completed, truncated, failed or cancelled. It
    holds
    the task id first, a *summary* and the *session usage*, and a failed result
    also holds its failure kind. The status truncated means only that the child
    stopped at its own length limit. The summary is the whole last assistant
    message of the child run.

    A session usage is the token count, the cost and the context use at settle
    of a
    child run, as its driver reports them. Each field is optional, and a field
    that
    the driver cannot report is unavailable and never zero.

    A *launch spec* is the record of how a driver started a child run, without
    its
    prompt: the command as one opaque list, the *child working directory*, the
    names of
    its
    environment variables and never their values, the version of the child
    program, the skill folder paths and the thinking level that the driver set
    for
    the child run. A field that the driver cannot report is unavailable.

    A *domain event* is one step of a child run, such as run started, skills
    verified, tool activity, agent message, turn settled and run exited. Tool
    activity carries the tool name and its target path, and the run started
    event
    carries the launch spec of the child run. Each domain event is written to
    the
    event log of its task.

    A *child session* is the record of the child's own conversation that its
    child driver keeps for a task, in the sessions folder that TaskStore
    provides.
  }

  logic {
    On a task request, create the task with a new task id, the frozen
    agent definition, the brief and the enqueue time, and write the task record
    with the status running, the model endpoint of the agent definition and the
    enqueue time. The task id goes to the result and to the child port, and the
    agent definition goes to the child port with the project root, the
    sessions folder and the folder of the driver files of the task. The
    task record
    goes to
    the final
    step, which writes the result into it. Keep the child run Pending until its
    caller releases it. On release, take the start time, write it into the
    task record and go on to the brief check. The brief check confirms that the
    task holds exactly one brief and passes that brief to the child port, which
    starts the child run and ends this part of the flow.

    While the child run is live, write each domain event to the event log and
    offer
    it to the caller, which ends that branch. When the run started event
    arrives,
    write the launch spec it carries into the task record without reading it,
    which
    ends that branch. When the child run settles, build the result with the
    task id
    first, the whole last assistant text as the summary and the session usage.
    The result
    goes to the final step. A failed child run builds its result with its
    failure kind, and a cancelled child run builds it with the status cancelled
    and
    no failure kind. Both go to the same final step. In the final step, write
    the
    result into the task record and return the result to the caller, which ends
    the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    A task has at most one live child run, and a task request produces exactly
    one
    result.

    TaskCore must reach a child only through the *child port* and must not
    import
    types specific to one child host. TaskCore requires the child port of the
    child host of the
    agent definition, which a child driver provides and its caller hands over,
    and
    does not depend on any particular child driver.

    TaskCore requires the task record and depends on TaskStore, which provides
    it. Only TaskCore may ask TaskStore to write a task record, and TaskStore
    performs the write.

    The result must not carry the *transcript*, *tool output* or *skill content* of
    the
    child run.

    TaskCore must not record or compute which files a child run changed, must
    not
    snapshot or copy *workspace files* and must not gate writes. The child run is
    limited only by its tool list, and what it writes is for the user to review
    with git, including when a parent model and a child write the same files.

    A cancelled child run that is still Pending must never start a child. It
    has the status cancelled, no failure kind, no session usage and no summary.

    The summary must never be cut, whatever its length or status, and an empty
    summary stays empty. TaskCore holds no size for a summary: a front end that
    knows how its parent host receives a result may tell the child a size in
    the
    brief.

    A field of the session usage that the child run cannot report must be
    marked
    unavailable and never reported as zero.

    A launch spec must never hold the value of an environment variable, because
    the task record is plain text, and TaskCore must write the launch spec into
    the
    task record unread and must not depend on any flag that it holds.

    The start time must be taken when the caller releases the child run and
    never read from the launch spec, and a child run that is still Pending
    has no start time.

    TaskCore owns one brief for each task in the spike, so continue, ask, fork
    and
    rerun are not offered.
  }

  cases {
    A summary of 60,000 characters, or of a truncated result, is returned whole
    with its status unchanged, and the task id is still first in the result.

    A child run whose last assistant message has the stop reason error is
    Failed
    with the failure kind error, and a cancelled child run carries no
    failure kind.

    The run started event of a child run puts its launch spec in the
    task record
    before the child run settles.

    A task whose child run stays Pending longer than its timeout still runs
    once
    it is released, and its start time is later than its enqueue time.
  }

  interface {
    Accept a task request made of an agent definition, a brief, the child port
    of
    the child host of the agent definition and an optional parent session id.
    Return the
    result, which never carries the transcript, tool output or skill content, with
    the task id first once the child run settles, or let the caller leave the
    task
    running and ask for its result later. A child run stays in the
    child run state Pending until its caller releases it, so that the caller
    can
    limit how many child runs run at the same time.

    Offer the child run state, the enqueue time and the start time of a live
    task to the caller, so that it can tell a Pending child run from a released
    one.

    Cancel a live child run as cancelled, and return the result.

    Offer each domain event of a live child run to the caller as it happens,
    and
    offer a failure kind in the result of a failed child run.

    Define the child port that a *child driver* implements: start a child run
    for a
    task id, an agent definition, a brief, the project root, the
    sessions folder
    and the folder of the driver files of the task, so that a child driver
    resolves
    none of these itself and reads no task record. Hand its launch spec, including
    its child working directory, to the
    caller in the run started domain event, run it to settlement, cancel it,
    and
    report the outcome, which is the last assistant text, the status, the
    failure kind of a failed run and the session usage.

    A child driver may also offer an *attach command* that reopens the
    child session of a task in the terminal interface of its child program,
    built
    from the launch spec of the task, a *session export* that writes the
    child session of a task as a document that a person can read, and its
    *starter tools*, which are the tool list that suits a general agent on its
    child host
    and the tool list that only reads and suits a fallback agent on its
    child host,
    and
    a *prerequisites check*, which passes or returns a message that says what
    its
    child host lacks and what to do. A caller must ask for any of the four
    through
    the
    child driver of the child host of the task or of the agent, and must not
    assume
    that a child driver offers it.
  }

  decisions {
    The spike keeps one brief per task because each interpreter call is a fresh
    single request, and the experiment does not need follow-up turns. Cancelled
    is
    a status and not a failure kind, because a failure kind belongs to a failed
    result that a parent model received. The launch spec is a shape of TaskCore
    and
    not
    of any child driver, so that TaskCore, TaskStore and Cli carry no driver
    type.
    The summary is never cut, because a cut answer or cut JSON fails the
    parent model
    in a way that depends only on how long the child wrote, and the size a
    parent model
    can take inline belongs to the front end that returns it. TaskCore keeps no
    list of changed files because git is the record and the review of
    concurrent
    writers is the user's.
  }
}
